  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/9EAF43">Example <span data-tag="9EAF43">5.3.4.3</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a topological space, and let $\calC $ be the category of open sets of $X$ (with morphisms given by inclusions). Then an object $U \in \calC $ is compact if and only if $U$ is compact when viewed as a topological space: that is, every open cover of $U$ admits a finite subcover. </p>
